On Mon, 28 Jul 2003, Hoff Hoffman wrote:

> In article <3ifVa.3547$jD5.368601@dca1-nnrp1.news.algx.net>, "Dutchman2000" <icelord2006@yahoo.com> writes:
> :I've got an RL02 connected to my Vax 4000-200 through an RLV12 QBus
> :controller.
> ..
> :So my problem is with MOUNT, everytime I go to mount the drive the system
> :simply hangs and never seems to complete the mount, I end up having to CTRL-
> :BREAK to Halt the system back to Chevron and b up again from my DSSI DIA0:
> :
> :Anyone have any idea's what I'm doing wrong or what may be wrong???
> 
>   I will assume that the RLV12 interrupt vector is set per the OpenVMS
>   VAX host requirements -- and please note that the OpenVMS VAX SYSGEN 
>   utility doesn't display the actual settings for the interrupt vector,
>   it displays only the desired settings.
> 
>   This would appear to indicate a Q-bus level problem with the system.
>   The KA650 was rather too fast for the early revisions of the VCB02,
>   so I can easily believe a yet-newer CPU having bus lock-ups with an
>   even-more-ancient RLV12 series controller.
> 
>   AFAIK, the RLV12 has never been officially supported on a MicroVAX
>   class configuration.
> 
>   I'll assume you've seen the "serpentine" discussions around having
>   the correct Q-bus configuration and settings.  If not, please start
>   there -- please see Ask The Wizard (ATW) topics (1149), (1866) and
>   (3232), etc.
> 
>   Given the symptoms, I'd pull every Q-bus board out of the Q-bus and
>   verify the CSR and interrupt vector settings against those values 
>   that SYSGEN CONFIGURE has required -- see the ATW topics above for 
>   details.  I'd be looking for mis-set vectors, bus grant errors, etc.
> 
>   But again, as this is ancient hardware and is not (AFAK) supported
>   in this configuration, it simply might not work -- the faster VAX
>   processors tend to push the Q-bus modules rather hard, and can tend
>   to be at the edge (no pun intended) of the Q-bus bus specifications.
> 
> :if it was a format issue or something, I could really use some help/advice
> :from anyone with a lot of experience with the quirks of the RL's.
> 
>   I'd be surprised if this were the volume format -- that class of
>   problem usually triggers a completely different error.
> 
> 
>  ---------------------------- #include <rtfaq.h> -----------------------------
>     For additional, please see the OpenVMS FAQ -- www.hp.com/go/openvms/faq
>  --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
>         Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoff[at]hp.com

Hate to dispute Hoff :-), but there may be a simpler explanation for
the symptom... If OPCOM messages are disabled for some reason (which
happens frequently if VMS decides you're on a workstation), and you
didn't use "/NOASSIST" on the mount, maybe it is complaining about
something trivial and you just aren't seeing the messages?

Is the system really locked up?  Can you get in from another terminal
or via a network connection?  If no network connections and only the
console terminal, you could try "$ spawn/nowait mount ..." so you can
watch it (show device, show process, ana/system, ana/error, etc.)

