Phillip Helbig---remove CLOTHES to reply wrote:

> I know the answers are there in the documentation, but there is A LOT of
> it!
> 
> Does anyone have a concise description of the principal differences
> between the BA350 JA, BA350 KF, BA350 SB, other BA350 models, BA356
> models and the BA353 (I know this is different physically, I mean other
> differences), in particular what types of disks one can put in them
> (size, 8/16 bit, part numbers etc).

The BA350 boxes are all basically the same.  The -?? variants tend to be 
packaging differences.  The -J? is just the box, while the -S? is the box 
with a rackmount kit, power supply, etc.  The -K? is a BA350 in a pretty 
desk-side pedestal.

The BA353 is basically a BA350 that only takes 3 drives, and only older 
drives at that.  No 7200 RPM drives, due to insufficient cooling.

There are 2 BA356s: the BA356 and the DS-BA356.  The BA356 is putty/beige 
in color, while the DS-BA356 is blue.  Again, the -?? variants are 
packaging descriptions (power supply or not, rack-mount kit or not, what 
type of personality module [if any], etc.).

The BA356 is a wide box, and can run at "fast" (10Mhz) speeds,. while the 
DS-BA356 is also wide and can run at "Ultra" (20Mhz) speeds.  

Both the BA356 and the DS-BA356 take one of several "personality" modules.  
These modules are where the signal cables connect.  There is a 8-bit 
module, a fast 16-bit module, several Ultra 16-bit modules (single ended, 
differential, one-bus, two-bus).

All the BA35[06] boxes are internally split bus.  Some of the variants come 
with a jumper (mounted on the backplane, in the back behind the fan) to 
join the two busses, so that you end up with a single-bus unit.  Others 
replace the jumper with a terminator so that you can have split-bus 
operation, with the appropriate personality module (in the case of the 
BA356 boxes).

You can NOT have wide disks in the BA350 or BA353, as they simply won't 
work.  You can have narrow disks in the BA356 boxes, provided the disk is 
sufficiently new (older disks would "lock up" the SCSI bus if plugged in).  
And, of course, you can have wide disks in the BA356 boxes.
