Do you have an Ethernet controller for it?  If so the easiest way to get an
OS is to get a cheap SCSI-based VAX or Alpha, get an OpenVMS Hobbyist Kit,
and boot the MicroVAX II over the net.

BTW, am I correct in assuming this is in a short 19" Rack?  I'm assuming it
is, since you mention you've got a 9-Track.  What kind of Hard Drives do you
have?

> Dan French
> P.S. is there _any_ (however slow) practical use for it once I get it
> working?

Believe it or not, it's a good system to learn to tune OpenVMS.  It is slow
enough that you'll be able to better notice improvements.  It's also a good
system to learn OpenVMS on.  They're also useful if you have a real need for
a Q-Bus Based VAX (I've got a MicroVAX III that I have for this reason).

Having said that, I'll admit I've converted my MicroVAX II (BA123 Chassis)
into a PDP-11/73, and am keeping a couple of VAXstation II's (a GPX and a RC
in BA23's) around for spare parts for my PDP-11/23+.  

