Not without some hacking. I did get tahoe running on a vax 4000/600,
which was easier since there is still a qbus and MSCP disks, the deqna/delqa
driver still works, etc etc. you'd have to write vsbus support and then
drivers for the ncr, the sgec, and the serial lines on the vs4k90 before
you can get anything interesting to happen.. i still had some adventures
getting it to run on the 600. even though the ka680 TM tried to assure
me that the qbus interface was identical to that in other qbus boxes back
to the ka650, in reality, the NVAX does some things very differently, which
breaks software that, like older unix, made some potentially unwise assumptions
about interrupts. i can give you my modifications to tahoe that work on
the 4k600 if you want. one thing also that was a shwostopper was the use of
EMODD in libc. NVAX follows the second edition VARM, with new rules on
instruction subsetting... EMODD is not supported, and it traps not to emulation
but to reserved instruction fault. so, i was careful and managed to
write emulation for it, stick that in modf.s somewhere in the libc
sources, and rebuild the library, recompile the compiler, recompile the
libraries again, and recompile the whole tree. the nice thing: it took
less than half an hour to compile the entire source tree! (kernel compiles
in 90 seconds! wow!)

it's pretty cool though, it feels incredibly fast compared to anything newer.
though, for functionality, youll feel some limits.. netbsd is a lot
more useful for day to day stuff than tahoe.

happy hacking,
isildur

